Drug-Induced Immune Thrombocytopenia (DITP)

### Patient Demographics
* Identifier: Patient H.L.
* Age: 58 years
* Gender: Male
* Weight: 80 kg
* Medical History: Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT), Hypertension (10 years), no baseline hematological abnormalities.

### Clinical Chronology
* Month 1 (Day 1): Diagnosed with acute lower-limb DVT; initiated on Unfractionated Heparin (UFH) continuous IV infusion (target aPTT 60 to 80 seconds). Baseline platelet count: 250,000 cells/mcL.
* Month 1 (Day 7): Patient developed sudden petechiae, ecchymosis at IV sites, and severe hematuria.
* Laboratory Findings at Day 7:
  * Serum Platelet Count: 22,000 cells/mcL (Profound drop of greater than 50 percent from baseline)
  * Hemoglobin: 11.2 g/dL (Baseline: 14.0 g/dL)
  * Serology: Positive for Anti-Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)/Heparin antibodies via ELISA.
* Intervention: Immediate cessation of Unfractionated Heparin on Day 7; switched to non-heparin anticoagulant (Fondaparinux); IV immunoglobulins (IVIG) administered.
* Follow-up (Day 14 post-discontinuation): Complete resolution of bleeding manifestations; platelet count recovered to 210,000 cells/mcL.

